diagnosis_text:
I verified end-to-end reachability between the “old side” and “new side” using the two representative hosts: pc0 (20.1.1.10) and pc12 (120.1.1.10). Both directions pinged successfully with 0% loss and ~2.8 ms RTT, so the halves are communicating. The inter-domain gateway in the middle (r11) is operating correctly: OSPF adjacency between r1 and r11 on B (111.1.1.x) is Full (r1 shows neighbor router-id 112.1.1.1 via eth1; r11 shows r1 as Full/Backup). EIGRP adjacency between r11 and r12 on EE (112.1.1.x) is also up (r11 sees neighbors 112.1.1.2 and 103.1.1.2). Routing confirms bidirectional knowledge: r1 installs 120.0.0.0/8 via OSPF through r11 (show ip route 120/8 -> 111.1.1.2), and r12 has a default route via r11 (kernel default via 112.1.1.1) for reaching the old side. Additionally, r1’s OSPF table contains EIGRP-originated 102.1.x/24 routes via r11, proving redistribution is functioning from new to old. 

Affected component: none currently; r11’s OSPF (area 3 on 111/8) and EIGRP 100 are healthy with redistribution into OSPF as configured. Mechanism: new-side prefixes are injected into OSPF, and new side reaches old prefixes via default to r11. Localization: r11 eth1 (111.1.1.2) and eth0 (112.1.1.1) adjacencies are stable. Root cause: no persistent misconfiguration present; any isolation reported last night appears transient and resolved. Corrective fix: no change required; recommend keeping OSPF↔EIGRP monitoring and, if desired, adding explicit OSPF-to-EIGRP redistribution on r11 for symmetry, though current default-routing suffices. 
=== END ===